Zune68 native compatibility and measurements
==========================================

Scope
-----
The native build targets AmigaOS 3.1+, a 68000 and no FPU. Classic MUI 3.8/3.9
application behavior is the priority. The bundled classes remain separate
libraries. Rawimage.mcc and Pixmap.mui provide the image classes used by
the built-in preferences pages. AROS module ports, muiscreen.library and
newer MUI features without an identified application need are deferred.

Version policy
--------------
The two native library names share library version [VERSION] and the
release [RELEASE] ([STAGE]). The library and built-in classes report
21.227, the version of MUI 5, which enables application version gates; it
does not claim complete MUI 5 compatibility. External MCCs retain their own
version reporting. The date identifies the Zune68 release.

Native version definitions are centralized in native_version.h. Building
with MUI5_TEST=0 gives AROS version 19.81 and built-in class version 1.1.

MUI 5 development scope
-----------------------
Implemented additions include library vectors, layout and pointers,
menu images and shortcuts, String editing, Title tabs, newer List/Text
APIs, Datamap, Objectmap, Textdata and Selectgroup. Data containers use
pools and binary-search lookup. Textdata borrows its source and owns its
converted buffers; codesets.library is optional. Without it, conversion
covers Latin-1, UTF-8 and big-endian UCS-2/UTF-16. Encoding conversion
alone does not add Unicode glyphs to the current Amiga font.

Dataspace Get/Count, directory byte totals through a 64-bit pointer,
Group/Family reordering, TextColor and Relayout are also implemented.
Window opacity is stored; OS3 does not gain composited transparent
windows. Default lists do not allocate column-mapping scratch space.

Filepanel/Fontpanel, separate-process MUI ASL requests, multi-file results,
user font families, Text marking and additional escapes, menu copying,
List column mapping and String hook actions are implemented. Process,
Keyadjust and Fontdisplay are available. Textdata_SourceEncoding matches
the observed no-op behavior of the 21.227 reference in the tested cases.

As in MUI 5, String implements the Textinput API: marks, clipboard and
edit methods, numeric input and the Textinput attributes. Textinput.mcc
and Textinputscroll.mcc 29 are thin wrappers. Multi-line editing is not
implemented; a Multiline String shows its text on one line.

Remaining gaps include Screenmodepanel, Slave, undocumented preferences
classes, complete theme compatibility and full application workflows.
Native ASL handles unsupported requester options. External MCC versions
and AfA OS interactions need their own application checks. See
tests/native/README.mui5.md in the source tree for reproducible probes,
reference differences and exact validation limits.

Implemented areas with isolated linked-code checks
-------------------------------------------------
- Public library gates, register hooks and classic structure prefixes
- Built-in and external class ownership, failure cleanup and expunge
- Notification removal, re-entry, source disposal and symbolic destinations
- Typed configuration lookup, numeric defaults and custom data pointers
- Buffered input, ARexx fallback hooks, AppIcon drops, legacy menus and help
- Floattext wrapping, dynamic group relayout and explicit cycle ordering
- Numeric scale intervals, reverse mapping and large-range arithmetic
- Signed String selection ranges and explicit up/down focus targets
- Bitmap remap ownership, image allocation failure paths and nested clips
- Pixmap raw/RLE/bzip2 decoding, data replacement and failed-allocation cleanup

These checks execute m68k code with modelled OS services. They are not an
AmigaOS application compatibility matrix or a physical-hardware result.
Use separate OS installations when comparing MUI and Zune68, particularly
with third-party MCCs which open muimaster.library themselves.

Rendering and lifetime limits
-----------------------------
Planar Bitmap rendering treats nonzero global alpha as opaque while
retaining its mask; zero alpha is transparent. Core RTG rendering
opens CyberGraphX V41 on demand and uses its classic pixel-array calls
for software alpha blending. Text RGB/ARGB and inline image pixels are
covered by a Picasso96 probe; complete RTG themes are not. Window refresh follows
the configured preferences. Both refresh modes use clip regions outside
an update, and managed refresh preserves pending damage while changing
regions. Broad redraw/text caches need measurements and GUI evidence
before adoption.

Rawimage.mcc and Pixmap.mui are separate, demand-loaded libraries. Pixmap
accepts RGB24, ARGB32 and CLUT8 images with raw, RLE or bzip2 storage.
Planar screens use graphics.library bitmaps, acquire only the pens actually
used, and approximate fractional alpha with a 4x4 coverage mask. They do
not require CyberGraphX. Truecolor rendering requires CyberGraphX v41;
its classic read/write API supplies software alpha blending.

Bzip2 scratch space is bounded by the declared uncompressed image size,
using the small-memory decoder. A 24x19 ARGB image needs about 70 KiB at
peak during decoding, rather than a 900 KiB block's full scratch space.
Decoded data is retained for reuse; bitmap, mask and pen ownership follows
the screen setup/cleanup lifetime. Borrowed raw image data remains owned
by the caller. Truecolor rendering needs more testing.

Notification list delivery tolerates callbacks removing notifications or
disposing a plain Notify source. An arbitrary class method may still need
its own instance after sending a notification. Defer disposal of such
objects through the application's pushed-method queue until the callback
unwinds. Explicit notification destinations remain caller-owned: remove
notifications before destroying independently owned targets.

Diagnostic TRACE=1 builds use a bounded 64 KiB T:zune.log fallback, with
messages truncated to 511 bytes and files closed after each write. Shell
output remains caller-owned. Use TRACE=0 for performance measurements.

TheBar
------
TheBar 26.22, TheBarVirt, TheButton, TheBar.mcp and the Toolbar wrapper are
built for the 68000 without an FPU and loaded only when requested. The five
stripped modules total 184,200 bytes. SDK headers, catalogs, documentation
and Examples/TheBarDemo are included. See Docs/thebar/Native for details.

On AmigaOS 3.2.3, TheBarDemo CHECK completes three construction,
attribute, window-open and disposal cycles. The preferences page renders
and Cancel exits with return code zero. This does not cover all optional
RTG effects or every legacy Toolbar application's behavior.

Application results
-------------------
IBrowse 3.0a basic functionality is working and the browser is pretty
usable. YAM 2.x, Voyager 3.2 and XSysInfo run as well. All 12 built-in
Prefs pages display images;
MCP pages appear once, and Prefs Test/Cancel returns successfully.

In IBrowse, page rendering, URL editing, links, HTTP downloads, tab
creation/switching/closing, bookmark navigation and menu Quit work.
Settings pages open and Cancel restores the underlying page. Native
list-tree glyphs and some settings layouts differ from original MUI.
Newstring.mcc clipboard behavior matches original MUI 3.9.

IBrowse 3.0a runs with Newstring 16.18 and with the String-derived 21.26
supplied with MUI 5. The latter requires muimaster.library version 20, so a
MUI5_TEST=0 build shows "Couldn't create CL_URLString" with it. Check both
MUI:Libs/mui and the browser's own mui drawer.

LumiZip 1.0.1 and LumiWeather 1.4.524 start and quit cleanly on AmigaOS
3.2.3 with networking off. A shutdown freeze reported on OS 3.9/AfA OS has
not been reproduced.

This is a beta, and there are many ways to improve it. Settings edits,
form handling, more applications, AmigaOS 3.1, 68000 machines and
CyberGraphX truecolor rendering all need more testing. We need testers:
please report what works and what doesn't.

Settings, Snapshot, Popup and Iconify title-bar buttons are implemented.
Iconify/restore and palette ownership work.

With the default smart refresh, the main windows of IBrowse, YAM and
Voyager restore completely when uncovered. After a resize, YAM's message
list area differs from MUI 3.9 in both refresh modes.

Application stress tests complete 1000 control and 1000 queued-call
lifetimes after warmup, with flat measured free memory/largest blocks
and no discarded-method callbacks. Window stress completes 100 cycles
with balanced setup/cleanup and stable measured memory and system colors.
One window test ended 112 Fast RAM bytes below its initial sample, so
window leaks are not ruled out. These results are not a general leak-free
claim.

Runtime workload
----------------
From an Amiga Shell, after installing and rebooting:

    Tests/compatcheck muimaster.library 100 >RAM:zune68-contracts.txt
    Tests/compatcheck muimaster.library 100 gui >RAM:zune68-gui.txt

The executable requests a 64 KiB stack. The optional gui mode repeatedly
opens and disposes a window, including group relayout. Run on a test
installation: these workloads exercise code which still needs GUI review.

The output separates deterministic 'contract' rows from 'metric' and
'memory' rows. It records notification values, numeric scaling, sorted
insertion, comparison calls, elapsed DOS ticks, free Chip/Fast RAM and
largest blocks. Numeric cases use observed MUI 3.8/3.9 results for shifted,
reversed and single-value ranges.
Two lifecycle iterations warm caches before the repeated-run baseline.
Library closure does not force expunge; the 'closed' sample may retain
idle classes or other OS resources. No automatic memory threshold is
claimed: other tasks, caches and fragmentation affect the measurements.

Repeat with 10, 100 and 1000 iterations and two simultaneous clients.
Record CPU/clock, OS, screen depth, RAM, compiler flags and exact binaries.
Compare trends, not a single free-memory sample. Existing opentest and
Prefs remain the interactive checks for keyboard focus, layout, clipboard,
MCP pages, Use/Save/Cancel, ARexx and Workbench integration.

Original SDK comparison
-----------------------
On the build host, point make at an extracted original SDK:

    make check-sdk MUI38_SDK=/path/to/MUI/Developer
    make check-sdk MUI_SDK=/path/to/SDK/MUI MUI_SDK_VERSION=3.9

This compiles the same client using the original public definitions and
regenerates only the obsolete GCC inline assembly from that SDK's FD and
prototypes. It leaves the original SDK untouched and does not redistribute
it. The outputs are build/muimaster/sdk-check/compatcheck-mui38 and
compatcheck-mui39, respectively. The MUI 5 SDK also works as the 3.9
SDK.

It then compares the value of every identifier that the SDK's
libraries/mui.h and mui/*_mcc.h define and Zune68 also defines, and
fails if any differ. Image and frame counts differ by design, so they
are listed but not counted as errors. tools/check_sdk_ids.py --missing
lists the SDK identifiers that Zune68 does not define.

Run that binary in separate MUI and Zune68 installations, then compare:

    python3 tools/compare_compat.py mui-contracts.txt zune68-contracts.txt

Matching contract output is one narrow result. Inspect timing/memory
separately and exercise real application/MCC versions before making broad
compatibility claims. Hosted GitHub Actions and physical hardware
still require their own runs.
